# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now — edit it, and shipshape will not
# overwrite it.
#
# Holds `an-operation-declares-its-permission`.
#
# **The permission is the class name, and there is no constant.** A `PERMISSION = :settle_invoice`
# beside `class SettleInvoice` is a second name for one thing, and two names that *can*
# diverge eventually do — one gets renamed and the other does not, and nothing fails. One
# way to say each thing: the class already says which act this is.
#
# **So renaming an operation renames the act, and that is a data migration.** It is not a
# hidden cost of deriving — it is the honest cost of the rename, which a separate constant
# only hides. `SettleInvoice` → `SettleBooking` means every stored grant of
# `:settle_invoice` becomes `:settle_booking`, in a migration written and reviewed like any
# other.
#
# **Every operation fails closed, and this is the point.** A new command's permission exists
# the moment the class does, and nobody holds a grant for it — so it is denied until someone
# grants it deliberately. There is no constant to forget, which means there is no way to
# write an operation that requires nothing. A permission model where the default is "no" and
# the exception is written down is the only one that stays correct as the codebase grows.
#
# **The catalogue is derived, never maintained.** Every operation answers `permission` and
# every workflow answers `permissions`, so the full set of permissions the system recognises
# is read off the classes — no seed file, no registry, nothing to fall behind the code. A
# checked-in list of permissions would be a copy of a fact the classes already state, and the
# copy is the one that rots.
#
# **A human-readable name is data.** "Settle an invoice", its description, who it is offered
# to — columns on the permission row, translated per locale and changed without a deploy. A
# display name in code would be the second name this module exists to refuse.
#
# **Uniqueness is free.** Two operations cannot share a permission because two classes
# cannot share a constant — Ruby holds it, so no guard has to.
module Permission
  # `Billing::SettleInvoice` → `:billing_settle_invoice`. Namespaced, because two contexts
  # may each own a `SettleInvoice` and they are different acts.
  def permission
    @permission ||= name
                    .gsub("::", "_")
                    .gsub(/([a-z\d])([A-Z])/, '\1_\2')
                    .downcase
                    .to_sym
  end

  # Raised where an operation answers no envelope — a query says what it found or raises,
  # so a refusal cannot come back as a value without every caller unwrapping one.
  class Refused < StandardError
    def initialize(permission)
      super("not permitted: #{permission}")
    end
  end
end
